Spiral Matrix

Intuition
To traverse an m x n matrix in spiral order, we can simulate walking along its outer boundary layer by layer in a clockwise direction: right, down, left, and up. By maintaining four boundaries (top, bot, left, and right), we can process the outermost perimeter and shrink the boundaries inward until all elements have been visited.

Approach
Initialize four boundary pointers: top = 0, bot = matrix.size() - 1, left = 0, and right = matrix[0].size() - 1.
Loop while top <= bot and left <= right.
Traverse from left to right along the top row, add the elements to the result vector ans, and increment top.
Traverse from top to bot along the right column, add the elements to ans, and decrement right.
Check if top <= bot still holds; if so, traverse from right to left along the bot row, add the elements to ans, and decrement bot.
Check if left <= right still holds; if so, traverse from bot to top along the left column, add the elements to ans, and increment left.
Return the result vector ans once all boundaries cross.

Code and Algorithm
class Solution {
public:
vector spiralOrder(vector<vector>& matrix) {
vector ans;
int top = 0, bot = matrix.size() - 1;
int left = 0, right = matrix[0].size() - 1;  

    while (top <= bot && left <= right) {
        for (int j = left; j <= right; ++j) ans.push_back(matrix[top][j]);
        top++;

        for (int i = top; i <= bot; ++i) ans.push_back(matrix[i][right]);
        right--;

        if (top <= bot) {
            for (int j = right; j >= left; --j) ans.push_back(matrix[bot][j]);
            bot--;
        }

        if (left <= right) {
            for (int i = bot; i >= top; --i) ans.push_back(matrix[i][left]);
            left++;
        }
    }
    return ans;
}
};

Time Complexity
O(m * n), where m is the number of rows and n is the number of columns, because every element in the matrix is visited exactly once.

Space Complexity
O(1) auxiliary space (excluding the output array ans) as only a few boundary pointer variables are used.

Why the optimal solution is better than the brute-force approach
A brute-force or visited-matrix approach often uses an additional O(m * n) boolean array to mark visited cells and dynamic direction vectors with collision checks. The boundary-shrinking approach manages the traversal boundaries directly in-place, eliminating the need for extra auxiliary memory, reducing branching overhead, and cleanly preventing out-of-bounds errors.
